INT. ROLLER RINK - AFTER HOURS - NIGHT

Disco ball turning slowly, scattering dull stars across a vast wooden floor. Neon trim hums. Rental skates in cubbies, laces tangled. The air smells of nacho cheese and old sweat.

DET. INGRID BOSTROM, 40s, trench coat, sensible shoes, stands at the rail. Her notebook is closed. She watches a lone skater glide in wide, lazy circles.

THE SKATER, 30s, a woman in a sequined jacket, moves like she's ice rather than wheels. Hair pinned up. A split lip she's trying not to touch.

BOSTROM
You skate well.

SKATER
Thanks.

BOSTROM
Better than my partner.

SKATER
He fell?

BOSTROM
He died.

The Skater keeps gliding. Her left wheel clicks once on a board seam. She doesn't look at Bostrom.

BOSTROM (CONT'D)
Where were you Thursday night?

SKATER
Here. I'm always here.

BOSTROM
Someone said that.

SKATER
Someone's right.

A door bangs at the far end. NIKO PAPADAKIS, 17, skinny, in a rental-desk polo, drags in a mop and bucket. He freezes at the sight of the detective.

NIKO
Sorry. I didn't know anyone--

BOSTROM
It's fine. What's your name?

NIKO
Niko.

BOSTROM
Niko, do you know this woman?

He glances at the Skater, then at the floor.

NIKO
That's Cleo. She teaches Tuesday nights.

CLEO
Niko, go home.

NIKO
Cleo, I--

CLEO
Go home.

He goes. The door swings shut. The disco ball keeps turning. Bostrom opens her notebook.

BOSTROM
Cleo what?

CLEO
Mbeki.

Bostrom writes it down. Cleo glides to the rail, takes hold of it, and for the first time looks her in the eye.

CLEO (CONT'D)
He was going to tell you.

BOSTROM
Who was?

CLEO
The boy.

Bostrom closes the notebook.
